#pragma once

#include "/Plugin/CloudAtmosphere/Private/GasGiantFlow.ush"

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Deck shadow map: the encoding, and the two sides that share it.
//
// INCLUDED BY BOTH THE BAKE AND THE MARCH, which is the whole point of the
// file. Every quantity describing the map -- its basis, its extent, the depth a
// sample sits at -- is derived from GGFieldParams and the light direction, and
// both sides already have those. Passing any of it as a parameter would create a
// second derivation that can disagree with the first, and a map sampled in a
// basis it was not written in fails silently: the shadows are smooth, plausible,
// and in the wrong place.
//
// THE ENCODING IS INVERTED. The map stores the DEPTHS AT WHICH FIXED OPTICAL
// DEPTHS ARE CROSSED, not the optical depth at fixed depths. That places the
// nodes by transmittance rather than by geometry, so they concentrate wherever
// the deck is dense -- the top of the density ramp, where view transmittance is
// spent, and where a slab fit anchored at the shell entry is worst. A deck top
// with near-zero density is what breaks the classic three-channel form.
//
// DEPTHS ARE OFFSETS FROM THE ANALYTIC SHELL ENTRY, in atmosphere thicknesses.
// Small and positive, so fp16 resolves a fraction of a gradient rather than a
// fraction of a planet radius, and the entry itself is recomputed in full
// precision on each side instead of being stored.
//
// OPTICAL DEPTH HERE IS ON THE FASTEST ABSORPTION CHANNEL. The bake folds
// cloudAbsorptionBeta's largest component into its accumulation, so the
// thresholds below mean transmittance directly and neither side has to agree
// about a scale factor beyond the beta both already read.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/** The four stored crossings. Spaced by what transmittance does rather than
 *  evenly: 0.25 is a 22% loss, 1.0 is 63%, 3.0 is 95%. The second is low on
 *  purpose -- it is what resolves the soft top of the ramp, which is the part
 *  the eye reads and the part a linear fit gets worst.
 *
 *  THE FIRST IS THE SILHOUETTE, AND IT IS A THRESHOLD RATHER THAN A GEOMETRIC
 *  ENTRY. A cone-traced deck top is a point sample of one column, so it cannot
 *  be averaged over the texel the way extinction can -- and being the shadow's
 *  leading edge, it is what a god ray draws. Point sampled, it snaps by a whole
 *  texel as the deck advects past the lattice. Taken as a crossing of the same
 *  subsampled accumulation as the others, it moves continuously.
 *
 *  Optical depth below it is treated as zero, which understates by at most
 *  GG_SHADOW_TAU_ENTRY -- under two percent of brightness, and smooth. */
#ifndef GG_SHADOW_TAU_ENTRY
#define GG_SHADOW_TAU_ENTRY 0.02f
#endif

#ifndef GG_SHADOW_TAU0
#define GG_SHADOW_TAU0 0.25f
#endif

#ifndef GG_SHADOW_TAU1
#define GG_SHADOW_TAU1 1.0f
#endif

#ifndef GG_SHADOW_TAU2
#define GG_SHADOW_TAU2 3.0f
#endif

/** Optical depth at which the tail extrapolation stops growing.
 *
 *  A CEILING, NOT A CUTOFF. Past the last stored node the reconstruction
 *  continues at the final segment's rate, which is unbounded on a dense column;
 *  this keeps it finite. It must not be used to snap transmittance to zero --
 *  a hard threshold on a texel-interpolated field draws an axis-aligned
 *  staircase along whatever contour it falls on. At 5 on the fastest channel
 *  the exponent is already under a percent, so there is nothing to gain. */
#ifndef GG_SHADOW_SATURATION
#define GG_SHADOW_SATURATION 5.0f
#endif

/** Written where a threshold is never reached, and where the ray misses the
 *  shell. Far past any real chord, so reconstruction reads zero rather than
 *  needing a validity test. */
#ifndef GG_SHADOW_NO_DECK
#define GG_SHADOW_NO_DECK 1000.0f
#endif

/** How far the map reaches past the cull radius. The limb is where the encoded
 *  depths vary fastest, and a map cut exactly at the shell puts that variation
 *  against the clamp edge with nothing outside to blend toward. */
#ifndef GG_SHADOW_EXTENT_MARGIN
#define GG_SHADOW_EXTENT_MARGIN 1.02f
#endif

/** Lookup offset, in texels, applied per view sample.
 *
 *  THE OPPOSITE CASE TO THE BAKE. A stochastic offset in the bake would print as
 *  fixed noise, because that lattice is shared by every pixel reading it and
 *  nothing averages it. Here the offset is per pixel and per sample along the
 *  ray, so the samples of one ray average the texel neighbourhood instead of
 *  reading one point of it.
 *
 *  It earns its place in the tangent case: a view ray running parallel to the
 *  light projects every one of its samples into nearly the same texel, so that
 *  texel alone decides the whole ray and its error reaches the screen as a block
 *  the size of a projected texel. Offsetting the reads is the only averaging
 *  available there.
 *
 *  1.0 is a full texel. Larger softens the shadow; 0 disables. */
#ifndef GG_SHADOW_LOOKUP_JITTER
#define GG_SHADOW_LOOKUP_JITTER 1.0f
#endif

/** The map plane's axes for a planet-local light direction, orthonormal and
 *  perpendicular to it.
 *
 *  ANCHORED TO THE SPIN AXIS rather than to an arbitrary perpendicular, so the
 *  grid does not rotate between frames as the light moves. A rotating lattice
 *  re-phases the reconstruction filter every frame, which shimmers at the map's
 *  resolution limit even when nothing in the scene has moved. The axis lands on
 *  V, putting the pole up the map.
 *
 *  (U, V, L) IS RIGHT-HANDED. Taking the cross the other way mirrors the map,
 *  which is invisible on a banded planet and surfaces much later as shadows on
 *  the wrong side of the lumps casting them. */
void GG_ShadowBasis(float3 LightLocal, out float3 OutU, out float3 OutV)
{
    const float3 L = normalize(LightLocal);

    float3 V = float3(0.0f, 0.0f, 1.0f) - L * L.z;

	// Light along the pole. Any perpendicular is as stable as any other there.
    if (dot(V, V) < 1e-6f)
    {
        V = float3(1.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f) - L * L.x;
    }

    OutV = normalize(V);
    OutU = cross(OutV, L);
}

/** The radius the map covers, which is the cull radius at UNFADED bounds.
 *
 *  PITFALL: this is NOT planetParams.cloudOuterRadius. That one carries the
 *  camera's layer fades folded in, so it shrinks as the structure layer fades
 *  out -- and a map written against one radius and read against another samples
 *  at the wrong scale, sliding the whole shadow toward the limb. Both sides call
 *  this instead. */
float GG_ShadowCullRadius(GGFieldParams F)
{
    float TopMin, TopMax;
    GG_TopBounds(F, 1.0f, 1.0f, TopMin, TopMax);

    return F.PlanetRadius + F.Thickness * TopMax;
}

/** Optical depth from one texel's nodes at a given depth.
 *
 *  Piecewise linear through the four stored nodes, monotone by construction
 *  because the nodes are sorted by the depth they were crossed at. Past the last
 *  node the final segment's rate continues: a sample there is deep inside
 *  saturated deck, so the value only has to stay large. */
float GG_ShadowTauFromNodes(float4 Node, float Depth)
{
    if (Depth <= Node.x)
    {
        return 0.0f;
    }

    if (Depth <= Node.y)
    {
        return lerp(GG_SHADOW_TAU_ENTRY, GG_SHADOW_TAU0,
			saturate((Depth - Node.x) / max(Node.y - Node.x, 1e-4f)));
    }

    if (Depth <= Node.z)
    {
        return lerp(GG_SHADOW_TAU0, GG_SHADOW_TAU1,
			saturate((Depth - Node.y) / max(Node.z - Node.y, 1e-4f)));
    }

    if (Depth <= Node.w)
    {
        return lerp(GG_SHADOW_TAU1, GG_SHADOW_TAU2,
			saturate((Depth - Node.z) / max(Node.w - Node.z, 1e-4f)));
    }

    const float TailRate = (GG_SHADOW_TAU2 - GG_SHADOW_TAU1) / max(Node.w - Node.z, 1e-4f);

    return min(GG_SHADOW_TAU2 + (Depth - Node.w) * TailRate, GG_SHADOW_SATURATION);
}

/** Optical depth between a planet-local position and the light, on the fastest
 *  absorption channel.
 *
 *  FOUR TEXELS RECONSTRUCTED, THEN BLENDED -- not one reconstruction from
 *  blended nodes. The node depths select which segment a sample falls in, so
 *  interpolating them first and branching after puts a slope discontinuity
 *  wherever a segment boundary crosses a texel. Those creases sit on the map
 *  lattice, which makes them look like resolution, but their width is set by the
 *  optical depth step across the boundary and does not shrink when texels do.
 *  Blending the reconstructed value is smooth by construction. */
float GG_ShadowTau(
	Texture2D ShadowMap,
	SamplerState ShadowSampler,
	float3 LocalPos,
	float3 LightLocal,
	float2 JitterTexels,
	GGFieldParams F)
{
    float3 U, V;
    GG_ShadowBasis(LightLocal, U, V);

    const float CullRadius = GG_ShadowCullRadius(F);

    const float2 Plane = float2(dot(LocalPos, U), dot(LocalPos, V));

	// (U, V, L) is orthonormal, so the in-plane offset IS the ray's closest
	// approach to the centre and its length squared is the perpendicular term.
    const float HalfChordSq = CullRadius * CullRadius - dot(Plane, Plane);

    if (HalfChordSq <= 0.0f)
    {
        return 0.0f;
    }

	// Depth from the shell entry, matching what the bake marched from.
    const float Depth =
		(dot(LocalPos, -normalize(LightLocal)) + sqrt(HalfChordSq)) / F.Thickness;

    const float2 UV = Plane / (CullRadius * GG_SHADOW_EXTENT_MARGIN) * 0.5f + 0.5f;

    uint MapWidth, MapHeight;
    ShadowMap.GetDimensions(MapWidth, MapHeight);

    const float2 Size = float2(MapWidth, MapHeight);
    const float2 InvSize = 1.0f / Size;

	// Texel centres, so the bilinear sampler returns each texel's own value.
    const float2 Centre = UV * Size - 0.5f + JitterTexels * GG_SHADOW_LOOKUP_JITTER;
    const float2 Base = floor(Centre);

    float2 Frac = Centre - Base;

	// QUINTIC WEIGHTS, NOT LINEAR ONES. Straight bilinear is continuous in value
	// but not in slope: its gradient jumps at every texel boundary, and across a
	// steep optical depth gradient those jumps read as facet creases locked to
	// the lattice -- which is why they halve exactly when the map doubles while
	// blending the value alone does not touch them. This has zero derivative at
	// both ends, so the boundaries stop being visible at all.
	//
	// Texel centres are unaffected, so nothing is blurred that was not already
	// being interpolated.
    Frac = Frac * Frac * Frac * (Frac * (Frac * 6.0f - 15.0f) + 10.0f);

    const float2 UV00 = (Base + 0.5f) * InvSize;

    const float4 N00 = ShadowMap.SampleLevel(ShadowSampler, UV00, 0.0f);
    const float4 N10 = ShadowMap.SampleLevel(ShadowSampler, UV00 + float2(InvSize.x, 0.0f), 0.0f);
    const float4 N01 = ShadowMap.SampleLevel(ShadowSampler, UV00 + float2(0.0f, InvSize.y), 0.0f);
    const float4 N11 = ShadowMap.SampleLevel(ShadowSampler, UV00 + InvSize, 0.0f);

    const float T00 = GG_ShadowTauFromNodes(N00, Depth);
    const float T10 = GG_ShadowTauFromNodes(N10, Depth);
    const float T01 = GG_ShadowTauFromNodes(N01, Depth);
    const float T11 = GG_ShadowTauFromNodes(N11, Depth);

    return lerp(lerp(T00, T10, Frac.x), lerp(T01, T11, Frac.x), Frac.y);
}
